  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'schemabench_0103' consists of 8 tables. The tables are defined as follows:\n1. `category`: This table represents the categories of charges. It has three columns: `category_no` (an integer, primary key), `category_desc` (a string of up to 31 characters, not null), and `category_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null).\n2. `charge`: This table represents the charges associated with members, providers, and categories. It has nine columns: `charge_no` (an integer, primary key), `member_no` (an integer, not null), `provider_no` (an integer, not null), `category_no` (an integer, not null), `charge_dt` (a datetime, not null), `charge_amt` (a decimal number with 19 digits, 4 of which can be after the decimal point, not null), `statement_no` (an integer, not null), `charge_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null), and foreign keys to `category`, `member`, `provider`, and `statement` tables.\n3. `corporation`: This table represents the corporations. It has eleven columns: `corp_no` (an integer, primary key), `corp_name` (a string of up to 31 characters, not null), `street` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `city` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `state_prov` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `country` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `mail_code` (a string of up to 10 characters, not null), `phone_no` (a string of up to 13 characters, not null), `expr_dt` (a datetime, not null), `region_no` (an integer, not null), `corp_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null), and a foreign key to the `region` table.\n4. `member`: This table represents the members of the corporation. It has twelve columns: `member_no` (an integer, primary key), `lastname` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `firstname` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `middleinitial` (a string of 1 character, nullable), `street` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `city` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `state_prov` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `country` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `mail_code` (a string of up to 10 characters, not null), `phone_no` (a string of up to 13 characters, nullable), `photograph` (a binary large object, nullable), `issue_dt` (a datetime, not null), `expr_dt` (a datetime, not null), `region_no` (an integer, not null), `corp_no` (an integer, nullable), `prev_balance` (a decimal number with 19 digits, 4 of which can be after the decimal point, nullable), `curr_balance` (a decimal number with 19 digits, 4 of which can be after the decimal point, nullable), and `member_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null).\n5. `payment`: This table represents the payments made by members. It has six columns: `payment_no` (an integer, primary key), `member_no` (an integer, not null), `payment_dt` (a datetime, not null), `payment_amt` (a decimal number with 19 digits, 4 of which can be after the decimal point, not null), `statement_no` (an integer, nullable), and `payment_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null), and a foreign key to the `member` table.\n6. `provider`: This table represents the providers. It has eleven columns: `provider_no` (an integer, primary key), `provider_name` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `street` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `city` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `state_prov` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `mail_code` (a string of up to 10 characters, not null), `country` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `phone_no` (a string of up to 13 characters, not null), `issue_dt` (a datetime, not null), `expr_dt` (a datetime, not null), `region_no` (an integer, not null), and `provider_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null), and a foreign key to the `region` table.\n7. `region`: This table represents the regions. It has eight columns: `region_no` (an integer, primary key), `region_name` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `street` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `city` (a string of up to 15 characters, not null), `state_prov` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `country` (a string of 2 characters, not null), `mail_code` (a string of up to 10 characters, not null), `phone_no` (a string of up to 13 characters, not null), and `region_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null).\n8. `statement`: This table represents the statements of members. It has six columns: `statement_no` (an integer, primary key), `member_no` (an integer, not null), `statement_dt` (a datetime, not null), `due_dt` (a datetime, not null), `statement_amt` (a decimal number with 19 digits, 4 of which can be after the decimal point, not null), and `statement_code` (a string of 2 characters, not null), and a foreign key to the `member` table.\nAll tables have primary keys that uniquely identify each record in the table. The foreign keys in the tables are used to establish relationships between the tables, ensuring referential integrity. The `ON DELETE NO ACTION` and `ON UPDATE NO ACTION` rules mean that if a record in the parent table is deleted or updated, no action will be taken on the corresponding records in the child tables. The `ON DELETE CASCADE` and `ON UPDATE CASCADE` rules mean that if a record in the parent table is deleted or updated, the corresponding records in the child tables will be deleted or updated as well.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a credit management system to handle charges, payments, and statements for members associated with corporations. The system interacts with various entities such as members, providers, corporations, and regions to maintain comprehensive financial records.\n\nObjectives\n\n- To provide a centralized platform for managing financial interactions including charges, payments, and statements.\n- To ensure the accurate and efficient processing of financial activities.\n- To support automated reporting of member financial statuses.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Members: Individuals who receive financial services and have accounts in the system.\n- Providers: Entities offering services to members and receiving payments.\n- Corporations: Companies to which members may belong.\n- Administrators: Personnel managing system operations and ensuring data integrity.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n- Members can have charges recorded by providers across various categories.\n- Payments can be processed against member accounts, reducing outstanding balances.\n- Statements can be generated for members detailing charges and payments.\n- The system maintains a directory of providers, members, and corporations for reference in transactions.\n- Each financial transaction is associated with a unique statement for record-keeping.\n- Functional integration with database tables such as `category`, `charge`, `corporation`, `member`, `payment`, `provider`, `region`, and `statement` to track all financial activities.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- The system should ensure data consistency and integrity through proper use of foreign keys and constraints.\n- It must support secure and reliable operations with appropriate error handling for cascading deletes and updates.\n- The platform should be scalable to accommodate potential growth in the number of transactions and users.\n- It should provide timely and accurate financial reports to stakeholders.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0103.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0103\nCREATE TABLE `category` (\n  `category_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_desc` varchar(31) NOT NULL,\n  `category_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_no`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `charge` (\n  `charge_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `member_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `provider_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `charge_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `charge_amt` decimal(19,4) NOT NULL,\n  `statement_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `charge_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`charge_no`),\n  CONSTRAINT `charge_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`member_no`) REFERENCES `member` (`member_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `charge_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`provider_no`) REFERENCES `provider` (`provider_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `charge_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`category_no`) REFERENCES `category` (`category_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `charge_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`statement_no`) REFERENCES `statement` (`statement_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `corporation` (\n  `corp_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `corp_name` varchar(31) NOT NULL,\n  `street` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `state_prov` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `country` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `mail_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_no` varchar(13) NOT NULL,\n  `expr_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `region_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `corp_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`corp_no`),\n  CONSTRAINT `corporation_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`region_no`) REFERENCES `region` (`region_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `member` (\n  `member_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `lastname` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `firstname` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `middleinitial` char(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `street` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `state_prov` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `country` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `mail_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_no` varchar(13) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `photograph` blob DEFAULT NULL,\n  `issue_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `expr_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `region_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `corp_no` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `prev_balance` decimal(19,4) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `curr_balance` decimal(19,4) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `member_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`member_no`),\n  CONSTRAINT `member_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`corp_no`) REFERENCES `corporation` (`corp_no`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION,\n  CONSTRAINT `member_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`region_no`) REFERENCES `region` (`region_no`) ON DELETE NO ACTION ON UPDATE NO ACTION\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payment` (\n  `payment_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `member_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `payment_amt` decimal(19,4) NOT NULL,\n  `statement_no` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `payment_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`payment_no`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`member_no`) REFERENCES `member` (`member_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `provider` (\n  `provider_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `provider_name` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `street` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `state_prov` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `mail_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `country` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_no` varchar(13) NOT NULL,\n  `issue_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `expr_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `region_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `provider_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`provider_no`),\n  CONSTRAINT `provider_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`region_no`) REFERENCES `region` (`region_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `region` (\n  `region_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `region_name` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `street` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `state_prov` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `country` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  `mail_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_no` varchar(13) NOT NULL,\n  `region_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`region_no`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `statement` (\n  `statement_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `member_no` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `statement_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `due_dt` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `statement_amt` decimal(19,4) NOT NULL,\n  `statement_code` char(2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`statement_no`),\n  CONSTRAINT `statement_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`member_no`) REFERENCES `member` (`member_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
